## Configuration

Place the twenty centers at the vertices of a regular 20‑gon, i.e. at the points

$$p_k = (\cos(2\pi k/20),\;\sin(2\pi k/20)),\qquad k=0,1,\dots,19.$$

Equivalently, any twenty points in **strictly convex position** (all lying on the boundary of their convex hull, no three collinear) works. I claim every Voronoi cell of this configuration is infinite (unbounded).

## Key fact

**Lemma.** For a finite set $S$ of points in the plane, the Voronoi cell $V(p)$ of $p\in S$ is unbounded if and only if $p$ lies on the boundary of $\operatorname{conv}(S)$.

*Proof of the direction we use ($\Leftarrow$).* Suppose $p$ is a vertex of $\operatorname{conv}(S)$. Then $p$ is an exposed point: there is a unit vector $u$ such that $u\cdot p > u\cdot q$ for every other $q\in S$. Consider the ray $r(t)=p+tu$, $t\ge 0$. For any other site $q$,

$$|r(t)-q|^{2}-|r(t)-p|^{2}=|p-q|^{2}+2t\,u\cdot(p-q).$$

Since $u\cdot(p-q)>0$, the right‑hand side tends to $+\infty$ as $t\to\infty$. Hence for all sufficiently large $t$, $r(t)$ is strictly closer to $p$ than to any other site, so $r(t)\in V(p)$. The cell therefore contains a ray and is unbounded. $\square$

(The converse — an interior point has a bounded cell — holds because an interior point is surrounded in every direction by other sites, but we do not need it here.)

## Applying the lemma

For the regular 20‑gon (or any twenty points in strictly convex position):

- The convex hull of the twenty centers is the 20‑gon itself.
- Every one of the twenty points is a **vertex** of this convex hull, hence lies on its boundary.
- By the lemma, every Voronoi cell is unbounded, i.e. **infinite** (not enclosed on all sides by edges).

## Verification of the construction

Each vertex $p_k$ of a regular 20‑gon is exposed: the outward normal to the circumscribed circle at $p_k$ gives a direction $u_k=p_k$ (viewed from the origin) with $u_k\cdot p_k = 1 > u_k\cdot p_j = \cos(2\pi(k-j)/20)$ for all $j\neq k$. So the ray from $p_k$ radially outward lies entirely in $V(p_k)$ beyond some finite radius, confirming each cell escapes to infinity along its own outward radial direction.

Thus twenty centers placed at the vertices of a regular 20‑gon form a configuration in which **all twenty Voronoi cells are infinite**.

### PROOF COMPLETE
